{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 replaces the skeptical fixed-point with a proof-theoretic grounded consequence relation whose extension is the set of formulas derivable in a monotone sequent calculus, claiming reinstatement is a derived theorem. However, reintatement as a *derived property* requires proving that whenever a former attacker is refuted, the previously suspended target becomes derivable without an explicit postulate. In a monotone Horn-fragment least Herbrand model, derivability is purely additive and never retracts, so the calculus has no native mechanism to show that the discharge of an attack *causes* a previously underivable node to become derivable. The target merely labels the attacker rule as 'discharges an attack when the attacker is refuted', but in a Horn setting this discharge is not a negation-as-failure or deletion operation; it is just another derivation step. Thus the target asserts reinstatement is derived but does not exhibit the derivation rule combination that actually yields it, leaving the guarantee unmet.","counterexample":[{"claim":"In a graph A->B->C where B is a committed root (so A is refuted and C is reinstated), SRC_001's Horn rules can derive A (attacker) and B (warrant), but there is no rule that takes 'A is refuted' plus 'B attacks C' to derive C unless a separate reinstatement axiom is added, which would make reinstatement a postulate rather than a derived theorem."}],"target":"8aca89816e1e72eb6fd8eb634f187334687b8c82e26451d4aed149a349b163ee"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"e48066634e08a782e602a14f1c9df54fc7bf18e92462feef04cc6c3837e39618"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 proposes computing skeptical intersection of all admissible fixed points via alternating graph reachability, claiming polynomial complexity because 'admissible sets form an antimatroid and membership can be decided by two reachability queries.' This is a mischaracterization: the family of admissible sets in abstract argumentation is generally not an antimatroid (the accessible union property fails for arbitrary attack graphs), and the decision problem 'is a node in every maximal admissible set' is equivalent to grounded extension membership only under the standard grounded semantics, not under SRC_003's redefined acceptance as intersection over *all* admissible fixed points. If the target truly means all admissible sets (not just maximal ones), the intersection is the *ideal* semantics, whose membership problem is not known to be polynomial. Thus the stated complexity guarantee relies on a false structural claim, violating the polynomial-cost preservation requirement.","counterexample":[{"graph":"A->B, B->A (two-node cycle)","note":"The admissible sets are {}, {A}, {B}. The intersection of *all* admissible sets is {}, but the intersection of *maximal* admissible sets is {A} ∩ {B} = {}. Here they coincide, but the claim that this is always decidable by two reachability queries on an antimatroid is unsupported; for graphs with reinstatement chains the ideal semantics differs from grounded and is harder."}],"target":"f4a0060ae701e465aee6ffc2e9a5426487239d3b1e179bc17d5c518de19e68b8"}]}